course: dictionary
lesson: 14830
title: Rabid: dangerous or extreme
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the sentence tells you which one is meant. Rabid has a serious meaning about animals and a lighter meaning about people's feelings. «The fox in the park was staggering and foaming at the mouth, so the warden said it might be rabid.» The clues staggering and foaming show an animal that is ill and dangerous.
explain: Rabies is a deadly disease. A rabid animal can pass it on, often by a bite, so never go near a wild animal that acts strangely. If any animal bites you, tell an adult straight away.
explain: When people say rabid about fans or opinions, they mean extreme and too strong in feeling. «My uncle is a rabid fan of the football team; he shouts at the TV in every match.» Nobody is ill here. The word shows that his feelings are very strong.
fact: Rabid means having rabies, a deadly disease that makes animals angry and likely to attack.
fact: Rabies is passed on, often by a bite, so do not touch wild animals that act strangely, and tell an adult if you are bitten.
fact: «A rabid dog» is a dog with rabies, and it is dangerous.
fact: «A rabid fan» means someone whose love for a team or star is extreme and very strong.
fact: The sentence is your clue: an animal acting wildly means rabies, while a person's love or opinion means extreme feelings.
exercise: «The rabid dog snarled at the gate.» What does rabid mean here? | *Has rabies, a deadly disease that makes animals attack | Hungry for its dinner | Very happy and wagging its tail | Very small and sleepy
exercise: «Dad is a rabid fan of the football team, and he owns every shirt.» What does rabid mean here? | Tired after a long game | *Extremely keen, with very strong feelings | Keen on cooking | Angry with the football team
exercise: What should you do if a wild animal bites you? | Ignore it and carry on playing | Wait to see if it bites again | *Tell an adult straight away | Give it a treat
test: What is the safest thing to do if you see a wild animal staggering and foaming at the mouth? | *Move away and tell an adult | Feed it some food | Go closer to take a photo | Pat it gently
test: Which sentence uses rabid to mean extreme and too strong in feeling? | The rabid rain fell on the roof | The rabid kettle boiled in the kitchen | *She is a rabid reader who finishes a book every day | Our rabid cat was asleep all day
exam: In «The rabid fox was foaming at the mouth», what clue shows the fox is ill and dangerous? | It was in the park | The grass was green | *Foaming at the mouth | Its bushy tail
